{
  "id": "Compound::DB00986 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Glycopyrrolate is implicated in the activation of Chrm1 through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Glycopyrrolate causes Cycloplegia, which is mediated by either Mepenzolate or Propantheline, both of which bind to Chrm1. This indicates that Glycopyrrolate exerts its effect via a common mechanism, likely by inducing a state of cycloplegia that leads to Chrm1 activation. The presence of Antimuscarinic Agents as common ancestors further supports a shared pharmacological profile, suggesting that Glycopyrrolate may act through a broader class of muscarinic receptor modulators. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Glycopyrrolate in modulating muscarinic receptor activity, with Chrm1 being a downstream target in the signaling cascade initiated by the drug.",
